Here is the commit message:
Call AbstractElement getID() insteado of getId()

Relying on capitalization significance to differentiate methods on
this object seems like asking for trouble.  For now, just fix the
capitalization bug, but we might want to
refactor getID() and getId() to more distinct names.

Here is the root cause:
The `AbstractElement` class (and its subclasses/interfaces) exposes two similarly-named but distinct methods—`getId()` and `getID()`—likely serving different purposes (e.g., `getId()` possibly returning an `Object`-based or Blueprints-compatible identifier, while `getID()` returns the internal primitive `long` ID used by Titan). Because Java method names are case-sensitive, these two methods are easy to confuse. In the `equals()` implementation, the developer mistakenly called `getId()` instead of the intended `getID()` when comparing `TitanVertex` and `TitanEdge` instances. This meant that equality checks relied on the wrong identifier accessor, potentially causing incorrect equality results (e.g., comparing boxed objects with `.equals()` instead of comparing primitive longs with `==`, or comparing IDs that are not guaranteed to be consistent/canonical for internal element identity). The underlying issue is the poor differentiation between two nearly identical method names, which allowed this subtle bug to go unnoticed.

Here is the patch:

     @Override
     public boolean equals(Object other) {
         if (this==other) {
             return true;
         } else if (other==null) {
             return false;
         } else if (this instanceof TitanVertex && other instanceof TitanVertex) {
-            return getId().equals(((TitanVertex)other).getId());
+            return getID() == ((TitanVertex)other).getID();
         } else if (this instanceof TitanEdge && other instanceof TitanEdge) {
-            return getId().equals(((TitanEdge)other).getId());
+            return getID() == ((TitanEdge)other).getID();
         } else {
             return false;
         }
     }



